model {
  extend basalt.toolsGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "Revoking a native grant records its pool cleanup in the tools host's auth store, then marks the grant revoked and deletes its access and refresh tokens, and only then asks the pool. The device's next access check, refresh, tool call and model request are refused whether or not the pool answers, and a revoke whose cleanup can't be recorded changes nothing. Once the grant is marked revoked, its isolate forgets every access answer it remembered for that grant, and keeps none from a check that read the grant's rows before then, so the device's next access check reads them again; a revoke made in another isolate reaches its checks within carry 0681's 3-second bound. The pool's part, the computer row and any model key at Home, is tried once in the revoking request; while the pool or Home does not answer, the revoke still succeeds and a server-only endpoint retries it every minute from Cloud's cron and self-host's background loop, least recently tried first, for 30 days. The retry removes a device only once its grant is revoked or gone, and drops a row it can't read. A pool that answers it holds no such device for the grant ends the cleanup: there is nothing left to clean. Connect revokes a native device by its computer at the tools host before it asks the pool, so a pool or Home that does not answer can't keep the device working; revoking it again records and tries its cleanup again, as does the person's revoke of a native grant already revoked. If the tools host doesn't answer, Connect's delete still takes the pool's path, and fails closed when the pool names a native grant. Reading more of a person's native grants than the lookup's cap refuses, since the computer's grant can't be confirmed absent. A device's own revoke needs only its bearer's token, grant and consent rows, not the pool or its tools membership. The same every-minute pass checks live tools-only native grants against the pool's existing check, since none of their own checks asks it: in grant id order from a saved cursor, at most 50 a pass, and a grant again only after a full cycle and at least 10 minutes since the cycle began. When the pool answers that the device's person revoked it, the pass revokes the grant and its tokens as any revoke does, in the same pass; a grant whose revoke fails is kept on a short retry list that the next pass tries first, so the cursor never passes a revoke that hasn't happened. Any other refusal, such as access removal or a person who left the tools organization, keeps the grant. While the pool does not answer, the pass changes nothing, logs one line and leaves its cursor for the next pass. The decide endpoint's cleanup of a lost approval race uses the same revoke. A failed grant record or a failed approval check cleans up at the pool inline and is not retried; no grant or token exists for that device. On the v2 pin the cloud auth wiring passes the native cleanups beside v2's usage-failure reporting."
    }
  }
}
